---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
---

<Layout title="What changes when you add optional: true to belongs_to?" description="Practice card. Two models, one option's difference. Drills the validation surface of belongs_to.">
  <article class="max-w-7xl mx-auto px-6 py-12 space-y-6">
    <div>
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors inline-flex items-center gap-2 mb-6">
        <span>←</span> Back to Course
      </a>
      <p class="text-sm uppercase tracking-wider text-ruby font-semibold mb-2">Practice · Reading the Source · Card 1</p>
      <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-ruby-dark mb-4">What changes when you add <code class="bg-gray-100 px-2 py-1 rounded text-3xl">optional: true</code>?</h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600">One option flag separates these two models. The visible difference is "must have a user vs not". The invisible difference is more interesting.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-2 gap-6 items-start">
      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6">
        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
            <span class="text-gray-500">🔍</span> The code
          </h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 italic mb-3">Same association. Different behavior. What did Rails wire up differently?</p>
          <div class="border-2 border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-gray-50/40">
            <CodeBlock code={`class Post < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user
end

class Comment < ApplicationRecord
  belongs_to :user, optional: true
end`} language="ruby" />
          </div>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3">The question</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">A Post without a user fails to save. A Comment without a user saves just fine. <strong>What did <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">optional: true</code> actually turn off?</strong> And how would you confirm it from the Rails console without trying to save a record?</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="bg-white rounded-lg shadow-sm border border-gray-200 p-8 space-y-6 lg:sticky lg:top-6">
        <div id="prompt-section" class="space-y-6">
          <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-lg p-7">
            <p class="text-base text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <strong>Take a moment.</strong> The difference isn't a separate validation in your model. It's something <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> itself installs. Name what it installs, and the global setting that decides whether it's installed by default.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="pt-2">
            <button
              id="show-solution-btn"
              class="w-full px-6 py-3 bg-ruby text-white rounded-lg hover:bg-ruby-dark transition-colors font-medium"
            >
              Reveal the answer →
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="solution-section" class="hidden space-y-6">
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span class="text-green-600">✅</span> The answer
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong><code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to :user</code> installs a presence validation. <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">optional: true</code> skips installing it.</strong> The validation isn't in your model file. It comes from the <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">Associations::Builder::BelongsTo</code> helper, which runs at boot time. You can see exactly which validators each model has:</p>
            <div class="border-2 border-green-200 rounded-lg p-4 bg-green-50/30">
              <CodeBlock code={`Post.validators_on(:user)
# => [#<ActiveRecord::Validations::PresenceValidator ...>]

Comment.validators_on(:user)
# => []

# Try saving:
Post.new.save           # => false, errors[:user] = ["must exist"]
Comment.new.save        # => true,  user_id is nil`} language="ruby" />
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>💡</span> The setting behind it
            </h3>
            <div class="space-y-3 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
              <p>Whether <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> installs the validation by default is controlled by <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">config.active_record.belongs_to_required_by_default</code>. In Rails 5+, the new-app default is <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">true</code>. In apps upgraded from Rails 4, it's often still <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">false</code>.</p>
              <p>This is why two Rails apps behave differently when you write the same <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to :user</code>. The behavior isn't in the line; it's in the framework setting that line consults.</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-lg p-6">
            <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-gray-900 mb-3 flex items-center gap-2">
              <span>📚</span> Theory
            </h3>
            <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">For the full walkthrough of what <code class="bg-gray-100 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded text-sm">belongs_to</code> generates, read <a href="/lessons/source-belongs-to" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark font-medium">Card 1 of Reading the Source</a>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="flex justify-between items-center pt-4">
      <a href="/" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">← Back to Course</a>
      <a href="/lessons/practice-source-when-sql-runs" class="text-ruby hover:text-ruby-dark transition-colors">Card 2 →</a>
    </div>
  </article>

  <script>
    const btn = document.getElementById('show-solution-btn');
    const prompt = document.getElementById('prompt-section');
    const solution = document.getElementById('solution-section');
    if (btn && prompt && solution) {
      btn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        prompt.classList.add('hidden');
        solution.classList.remove('hidden');
        if (window.innerWidth < 1024) {
          solution.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
        }
      });
    }
  </script>
</Layout>
